#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Analyse one archived run: what got done, where the time went, and tokens.

usage:
  python3 autopsy RUN_FOLDER [--quiet]

RUN_FOLDER is a history run folder (archive-run's layout): a *.lanes.json
manifest, wf_<id>/ transcript dirs each with its wf_<id>.json result file,
the *.ledger dir and the run report (the first of *.report.json,
*.run-report.json and report.json). autopsy reads only that folder, never a
live path from the manifest, so imported runs and runs from another OS work.
It writes RUN_FOLDER/autopsy.json and RUN_FOLDER/autopsy.md (replacing only
those two files) and prints a summary of at most 15 lines to stdout:
the wall time, for a run with more than one launch the line 'earlier
launches: kept <N> agents, redid <M> (<X> of agent time)' (the earlier
launches' kept_agents, redone_agents and redone_agent_s summed, and
'(agent time unavailable)' in place of '(<X> of agent time)' if any is;
see launches), when the user accepted the delivered head and the run's
own verdict is not accepted the line 'acceptance: <status>; accepted by
the user at the delivered head' (see done), the slowest phase, the line
'longest on the critical path: <label> (<s>)' when time.critical_path's
longest is set (see time), the top
three slowdowns with their cost, when a recorded launch has a warned,
failed or unread check the line 'launch conditions: <n> check(s) warned
or failed before launch[, <k> could not be read]; <m> possible
contributor(s)' (see launch_conditions), the line 'project test runs:
<P>[ (+<N> unavailable)]; check calls with a test group: <T>[ (<role>
<n>, ...)]' (see checks: P is project_test_runs' total, with N its
unavailable count when above 0; T and the roles are the legacy
full_suite_runs'; each reads unavailable when its source is), the total
tokens (a minimum when some usage is missing, unavailable when no agent
has usage), the top suggestion and the absolute path of autopsy.md.
--quiet prints nothing.

autopsy.md is plain ASCII with these sections, in order: Summary (with
the earlier launches line as '- Earlier launches: ...', the acceptance
as '- Acceptance: <status>' and, after '- Slowest phase: ...', the line
'- Longest on the critical path: <label> (<s>)' when time.critical_path's
longest is set), What got done (its '- Acceptance: <status>
(<kind>: <class>, ...); warnings <n>' line and the Summary's both end with
'; accepted by the user at the delivered head' when accepted_by_user is
true and the status is not accepted), Where time went (for a run with
more than one launch, first a launches table '| Launch | Status | Wall |
Kept agents | Kept | Redone agents | Redone |' with Kept and Redone the
kept_s and redone_s; then a phase
table, a 40-column text timeline per lane and the critical path, marked
as an estimate), Launch conditions, Checks, Slowdowns, Tokens, Suggestions
and Open questions (one line per unexplained entry). It holds no free text
from the transcripts or doctor's details.
Launch conditions opens with 'Observed before each launch. A condition may
not have lasted through the run, and a warning beside a slowdown is a
possible contributor, not a cause.' A recorded launch gets 'Observed before
launch <wf> (<N> min before its start), doctor <version>: <status>' (N in
whole minutes, rounded down), a '- <check>: <outcome>[ (<detail>)]' line
per warned or failed check that was read, '- <check>: could not be read'
per unread one, 'Skipped: <check> (<reason>), ...' and 'Possible
contributors: <check> (<kind> xN, ...), ...' or 'none'. Any other launch
gets '<wf>: <reason>', and each orphan snapshot 'Snapshot for a launch not
in this archive: <wf>'.

autopsy.json:

  {autopsy_version, inputs_fingerprint, run_id, engine_version,
   launches: [{wf, start, end, wall_s, status, kept_agents, redone_agents,
               kept_s, redone_s, redone_agent_s}],
   done, time, tokens, checks, slowdowns, detectors, suggestions,
   unexplained, launch_conditions, orphan_snapshots}

inputs_fingerprint: sha256 over the sorted [relative path, size, sha256] of
every file autopsy reads (the manifest, report, wf_*.json, wf_*/* and the
ledger files); it changes when any input changes.

launches: one per wf_* dir, in startTime order. start and end come from the
result file's startTime and durationMs; without them, from the launch's
agents' first timed line and last event. status is the result's status.
An agent of a launch but the last is redone when a later launch ran an
agent for the same work: an agent with a task (time.agents' task not
null), when a later agent has the same task and the same role; one
without a task (pre-flight, integrate, verify, final review <lens>, merge
agents and the like), when a later agent without a task has the same
role. Else it is kept, as is every agent of the last launch. Matching is
exact on the labels' task and role: a batch unit (A1-A3) a later launch
runs as another unit (A2-A3) never matches, and neither do launch 1's
'wave 1 merge' and a later 'recovery merge'. kept_agents and
redone_agents count them; kept_s and redone_s are the wall time the kept
or redone agents covered (the union of their spans), redone_agent_s the
redone agents' summed wall times (the relaunch row's span_s); each is
"unavailable" when one of its agents is untimed, and 0 with no agent.

done (from the last launch's result): tasks {<id>: {status, commits,
range, rounds, tier_used}}, acceptance {status, reasons: [{kind, class}],
warnings: <count>, accepted_by_user}, final {fixed, declined, open}
(counts) and e2e (pass, fail or none). Free text (notes, reason details,
warnings, findings, evidence) is never copied.
- tasks.commits is the task's commit count from the ledger: the distinct
  SHAs in the commits lists of its committed and settled events (one per
  commit round) in every *.jsonl of the ledger, after its last started
  event with restart true (or from the start without one); a plain
  started (a retry or a rerun) does not reset it. A batch's events repeat
  the batch's commits under each task, so each counts the batch's. It is
  "unavailable" without a readable ledger, when the task has no such
  event, or when one of them has no commits list of SHAs (an older
  ledger); lines that are not JSON objects are skipped. range is the
  result's own commits, the [base, head] pair the engine writes, when
  both are SHAs, else null. What got done prints 'N commits', '1 commit'
  or 'commits unavailable'.
- acceptance.status stays the run's own verdict. accepted_by_user is true
  when the ledger's _run.jsonl holds an accepted event (what 'ledger
  accept' appends) whose head equals the result's delivered_sha exactly,
  as audit compares; with several events, when any does. It is false when
  none is at that head, and "unavailable" without a ledger dir, without a
  _run.jsonl in it, or without a delivered_sha of 7-64 hex digits. Lines
  that are not JSON objects are skipped. Only the boolean is copied, never
  the event's text (it names a person) or its head.

time: wall_s (the launches' sum), phases [{phase, launch, start, end,
wall_s, agents}], lanes [{lane, launch, start, end, busy_s, idle_s}],
agents [{label, launch, phase, task, role, start, end, wall_s, tool_s,
tool_s_min, other_s, unmatched_calls}], critical_path {estimate: true,
labels, longest} and idle_gaps [{start, end, s}].
- An agent's span is its first timed transcript line (the prompt
  included) to its last event (assistant message, tool call or tool
  result). tool_s_min is the union of its matched tool call spans
  (tool_use to the tool_result that answers it), so overlapping calls
  count once; unmatched_calls counts its tool calls that no tool_result
  answers. tool_s is its tool time and other_s the rest (model time,
  transport and scheduling). An agent is partial when it has an unmatched
  call or unreadable transcript lines: a lost result may hide a call that
  ran to the agent's end, so its tool_s and other_s are "unavailable" and
  tool_s_min is a lower bound (autopsy.md shows '>= N'). Otherwise tool_s
  is tool_s_min, and a timed agent with no calls has a measured tool_s of
  0. An untimed agent has all four "unavailable", and unmatched_calls too.
- Phases come from each agent's workflowPhase, per launch. A phase's start
  and end are its timed agents'; its wall_s is "unavailable" unless every
  agent of it is timed. A lane is a phase named as a manifest lane's name,
  or 'Lane <id>'; busy_s is the union of its agents' spans and idle_s the
  rest of its span (both "unavailable" unless every agent is timed).
- The critical path is a timing-based estimate: from the last-ending agent,
  walk back to the latest-ending agent that ended before it started.
  longest is {label, launch, wall_s} of the longest timed agent on it,
  taken from the agents themselves (a label repeats across launches and
  reviews), the first on the path on a tie; null when no agent on it is
  timed (the summaries then have no line about it).
- An idle gap is a span over 60 s inside a launch where no agent of that
  launch runs. A launch with an agent that has no timestamps has none
  listed (and with no such launch the idle_gap detector is
  not_available): its gaps cannot be told from missing timestamps.

tokens: totals, by_phase, by_role, by_tier and per_task buckets in
run-report's token-field shape (input_tokens, output_tokens,
cache_read_input_tokens, cache_creation_input_tokens, output_tokens_min,
agents) and rules, plus agents_with_usage: the bucket's agents whose usage
is known. A bucket where no agent has usage (agents_with_usage 0) has every
token field and output_tokens_min "unavailable", never 0; a partly covered
bucket keeps its sums, which are lower bounds. top_agents (the five
largest by input + cache creation + output, output_tokens_min when output
is incomplete); unreadable_lines (as run-report counts them); partial:
true when any agent is output-incomplete, has unreadable lines or has no
usage at all, so the totals are lower bounds. With no usage in the whole
run, autopsy.md and the summary say the totals are unavailable.

Background runs. A Bash call went to the background when Claude Code's
notice on the first line of its result says so ('Command running in
background with ID x', or '... was moved to the background (ID: x)' at a
timeout). Its completion is observed at the earliest of, at or after that
result: Claude Code's own task notification for x (a transcript line of
type attachment with commandMode task-notification, whose task-id is x and
whose status is not running or pending); the result of a BashOutput or
TaskOutput call for x whose first <status> element is completed, failed or
killed (a later one is the task's own output); the result of a TaskStop or KillShell call for x that is no error.
That time is an upper bound on the real finish. Notification-shaped text
in a user line or in any tool result never counts: an agent can read such
text from a file.

slowdowns: [{kind, agent, at, cost_s, cost_kind, evidence, span_s, merged,
contains}, and background_s for a background_check], largest cost_s
first. agent is the label, or null for idle_gap (run-wide) and relaunch
(a launch's row, over its redone agents). cost_kind is measured, bound when the
end is an upper limit, or unavailable when the end is not known (cost_s is
then "unavailable"). cost_s is the row's charged time and span_s its time
before charging (for a background_check, the agent's waiting); merged
counts the detections the row holds (1 for a lone one) and contains lists
the kinds folded into it, sorted (see One second, one row below).
evidence is {tool, exit, marker, program, check}: the tool name, the exit
code a result reports (or null), the marker string matched (from the table
in _autopsy.py, or null), the program a Bash command ran, as a basename,
and the id of the check the call's line runs, as checks names it (such as
test#1, <lane>.test#1, run-checks or task-checks), or null; the call may
not have reached it (see certainty in checks). The line is read in
simple commands as check matching reads it (leading keywords, NAME=value
assignments and prefixes such as env, 'timeout DURATION' or npx skipped;
'cd DIR', 'export ...', a loop header, a closing keyword, assignments alone
or a case pattern run no program), and the program is, by the first rule
that applies: (1) when the line runs a check, the program of the simple
command that runs it; (2) when the line holds a loop (for, while or until)
and a simple command runs sleep, sleep (a wait loop); (3) the first program
that is not plumbing (cat head tail grep ls wc echo pwd which true false
printf test [ [[ ]] seq read break continue sort uniq cut tr tee sed awk rm
mv cp mkdir mktemp touch date git sleep), else the first program. A
program is its simple command's first word; an interpreter (python*, py,
bash, sh, with .exe) followed by a script word, past its options, gives the
script's basename, and followed by -c, -m or - (or no script word) is the
interpreter itself; a bare variable ($NAME or ${NAME}) followed by a script
word gives the script's basename, else null, and a word that starts with a
variable and holds a path ($VENV/bin/x) is a path like any other. A script
word holds '/' or '\\', or ends in .py, .js, .mjs, .cjs, .sh or .ps1. A word
with '*' or '?' (a case pattern) is never a program or a script word. The
program is null when it holds '=', '@' or ':'. A line whose quoting does
not parse is read as plain words, which may split quoted text: its program
is the first simple command's, with none of the rules above. No command,
output or prompt text is written. A call fails when its result is an error or starts with
an 'Exit code N' line with N not 0. A call's time starts at its own start:
the later of its tool_use and the latest result of an earlier call of its
model turn that came at or before its own result. The kinds:
- background_check: a Bash call went to the background (see Background
  runs); the time the agent waited inside the background window, from the
  call (from its result, after a timeout) to its observed completion
  (bound); background_s is the window's length. A wait call is an
  'await-checks wait' call, a BashOutput, TaskOutput or Monitor call, a
  Bash call that runs sleep in a simple command, or a Bash or Read call
  that names a harness tasks/<id>.output file. The waiting is the union,
  clipped to the window, of (a) each later wait call, from its own start
  to its result; (b) the gap between two calls next to each other when the
  second is a wait call and the first is a wait call or the backgrounded
  call (the model turns of a poll loop); (c) the stretch from the agent's
  last event to the completion when it is over 60 s and no call is open
  across it (the agent sat idle until the notice). The backgrounded call's
  own time is no waiting. A window without waiting is no slowdown (the
  agent kept working; checks.waits.background_not_waited counts it), and
  its call is no long_tool_call either. Without an observed completion
  its cost_s, cost_kind and background_s are "unavailable".
- timeout: Claude Code's notice starts a Bash result ('Command did not
  complete within its ...', 'Command timed out after'); the call's span.
  Text in command output, files read or other tools never counts.
- repeated_failure: the same Bash command (whitespace-normalised) fails
  twice or more in one agent; first failing call to last failing result.
- heredoc_failure: a failing Bash command whose heredoc file write failed.
  A heredoc write is a pipeline that is a heredoc feeding cat or tee, with
  no | stage, that writes a file (a > or >> redirect, or tee). It failed
  when the command is nothing but the write (each pipeline the write, or
  only 'cd DIR' or NAME=value assignments), or when the command holds a
  write anywhere and either (W1) it is at least 6,000 characters long (the
  Windows Bash tool cuts a command near 7,000 to 8,191) and its result,
  after 'Exit code 2', is only the outer shell's '<shell path>: -c: line
  N: unexpected EOF while looking for matching' line, optionally followed
  by the same shell's 'syntax error: unexpected end of file' line (bash
  5.1 and older), or (W2) its result holds the shell's 'line N: <target>:
  No such file or directory' for a > or >> target of a write, the target
  exactly as written, or by its basename when it holds $ or ~ (the shell
  prints the expanded path). A failing inline script such as 'python3 -
  <<EOF', even with its output redirected (> out.json), does not count,
  and neither does any other line that does more than the write (cat > f
  <<EOF then python3 f, or an inner 'bash -c', which names itself bash):
  its error text does not say which part failed, so it is an unlocated
  failure. From it to the agent's next successful file write, by Edit or
  Write or by a Bash redirect or tee (bound), else its own span.
- edit_retry: an Edit or Write fails as unread, changed, or text not
  found; from it to the next successful write of that file.
- extra_review_round: a task's 'fix N' and 're-review N' agents; the sum
  of their wall times, "unavailable" (cost_kind too) when one of them is
  untimed.
- escalation and retry: an agent run-report counts as one (the same label
  again at another model tier in the same launch, where a <synthetic> API
  error line never sets a model; a label ending ' retry'); that agent's
  wall time, "unavailable" (cost_kind too) when it is untimed. An
  escalation row also covers the review the engine runs again after it
  (an implement rerun at standard after a review's changes or a failed
  fix, then the whole task reviewed again): when the task's next agent in
  the launch's agent order is labelled exactly '<task> review' and an
  earlier agent of the launch has that label, that review's wall time
  joins the row ("unavailable" when one of the two is untimed). An
  implement escalated before any review of its task is followed by the
  task's first review, which stays out, and so does a review after an
  adjudicate.
- relaunch: every launch but the last, at its start; the summed wall
  times of its redone agents (see launches), "unavailable" (cost_kind
  too) when one of them is untimed, never 0 or a part, and 0 (listed,
  never folded) when no agent was redone.
- long_tool_call: a tool call over 120 s that no kind above counted, and
  never a run-checks or task-checks call (check time, below); a direct
  test command stays one.
- idle_gap: each time.idle_gaps entry.
- check_rerun: the same Bash command (whitespace-normalised) runs again in
  one agent, both runs certainly ran a check (certainty ran, see checks),
  and every call between surely changed nothing: a Read, Grep, Glob, LS,
  WebFetch, WebSearch, TodoWrite, BashOutput or TaskOutput call, or a Bash
  line that parses, writes no file (no redirect or tee), holds no $(,
  backtick, <( or >(, and whose every simple command (past leading
  keywords and prefixes) is cd or runs cat, head, tail, grep, ls, wc,
  echo, pwd, sleep, which or true. Any other program counts as a change
  (find, rg, sed, xargs, and every git command, since git can run
  configured helpers). The second run's span.
A cost that cannot be timed is "unavailable".

One second, one row: each second of an agent's time is charged to at most
one row. A background_check's span is the agent's waiting, not its whole
window. Rows of one kind and one agent whose spans overlap or touch merge
into one: at the earliest start, the span their union, cost_kind the
weakest, evidence the earliest row's (and background_s the length of the
union of the windows). Then, per agent, the rows take its
time in five tiers: timeout and background_check; check time,
long_tool_call and check_rerun; repeated_failure, heredoc_failure and
edit_retry; retry, escalation and extra_review_round; relaunch (a
launch's redone agents, so an agent both retried and redone goes to the
retry first, and a relaunch row covers each launch's own agents, so it
never merges with another).
Within a tier the shorter row (span_s) goes first, then the earlier, then
the order listed. A row takes only the parts of its span that no row
before it took: cost_s is their length. A row left with nothing is
dropped and its kind goes into contains of the row that took most of its
span (the earliest on a tie); a row of zero length goes to the earliest
row whose time, ends included, holds its instant, and with none it stays
at 0. Run-wide rows (idle_gap), a relaunch row with nothing redone (no
span) and rows of unknown cost (span_s "unavailable") are never merged,
charged or folded. autopsy.md shows a merged row's kind as
'<kind> xN', a cost below its span as '<cost> (of <span_s> s)', and the
check id as 'check <id>' and the folded kinds as 'contains <kind>, ...' in
its Evidence cell.
Check time (1.6.13): a run-checks or task-checks call with a result that
is no procedure execution (see checks.waits) is the check policy's own
execution, timed in checks. Its own time is charged as check time, an
internal row in the second tier before long_tool_call, so a window or
whole-agent row that holds the call (edit_retry, extra_review_round, ...)
keeps only its own time. A procedure execution's start and await-checks
wait calls with a result are check time too, each over its own time, so
a whole-agent or relaunch row that holds them keeps only the rest. Check
time is no slowdown: it has no row, suggestion, detector,
launch-condition contributor or history entry and is in no contains
(inside a timeout row of the same call it folds without a trace).
timeout and background_check still apply to a helper call that is no
procedure execution, and a call that is a check_rerun stays one (the
rerun row keeps its time).

detectors: {<kind>: ok | partial | not_available}. A detector is
not_available when its inputs are missing (no timed tool events, no
labels, no models, no timed launch); it then adds no slowdown. The tool
detectors (background_check, timeout, repeated_failure, heredoc_failure,
edit_retry, long_tool_call, check_rerun) are partial when any agent is
partial (see time): their slowdowns are kept, but they may miss some.
autopsy.md lists the partial detectors apart from those without data.

suggestions: [{kind, text, stake_s, count, slowdowns}], one per kind with a
slowdowns, worded as a likely cause and a change to try; stake_s is the time
its slowdowns with a known cost cover, per agent: the sum of their charged
cost_s (One second, one row), and slowdowns lists their indexes. Ranked by stake_s. When acceptance reports checks_unclean, an unclean_checks
suggestion (stake_s 0, no slowdowns) names the untracked files from the
saved verify evidence (<ledger>/checks/verify-*.json) by path and says to
commit any file a check needs and gitignore only known generated output.

unexplained: [{start, end, s, agent}], the five largest spans over 60 s of
an agent's time, cut at its events (a model turn, a tool call), that no
slowdown covers. A slowdown covers its own agent's time (a relaunch row,
its redone agents'), and an idle gap any agent's. Check time covers its
own agent's time too: a procedure execution's start and wait calls, and
the check time of helper calls (Check time, above).

launch_conditions: one entry per launch, in launches order: {wf, state,
reason, observed_at, before_start_s, doctor_version, status, checks:
[{check, outcome, detail, read}], possible_contributors: [{check,
slowdowns: {<kind>: <count>}}]}. It comes from the ledger's
doctor-<wf>.json snapshots (what 'ledger conditions' writes: {launch: {wf,
setup_event, setup_head}, recorded_at, doctor: <doctor's --json result>}).
Only a file named doctor-<wf>.json with <wf> matching
^wf_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$ is a snapshot, and it belongs to the launch its
file name names.
- state unavailable: no snapshot names the launch; reason 'launch
  conditions unavailable'.
- state inconsistent, with the first reason that applies: 'snapshot
  unreadable' (not a JSON object, no launch or doctor object, or an
  observed_at that does not parse); 'file name and content name different
  launches'; 'another snapshot names the same setup' (the same
  launch.setup_event; both are inconsistent); 'doctor result is for another
  run' (doctor.run_id is set and is not the manifest's run_id); 'launch
  time unavailable' (no launch start, or no end for the previous launch);
  'observed after the launch started'; 'observed before the previous
  launch ended' (in whole seconds).
- state recorded otherwise: reason is null, observed_at the doctor's
  observation and before_start_s the launch's start minus it.
For a launch that is not recorded, checks and possible_contributors are []
and the other values null: nothing from its snapshot is shown.
- Values copied from a snapshot are validated: a check entry is dropped
  unless its name matches ^[a-z_]{1,32}$ and its outcome is passed,
  warning, failed or skipped; status is ok or blocked, else null;
  doctor_version is N.N.N or 'unavailable', else null.
- checks come from doctor's checks list when present, else from its
  failures, warnings and skipped entries (so a passed outcome is never
  made up); when they disagree, the worst outcome wins.
- detail is derived per check, never copied: 'unknown' stays; a skipped
  check keeps its reason code ([a-z_]+); memory, heavy_processes and
  concurrency keep a detail of only letters, digits, spaces and . , ; ( )
  % ' _ -; disk keeps only '<N> GB free'; defender only 'covered' or 'not
  covered'; anything else is null.
- read is false for a warning whose detail is 'unknown' from memory,
  disk, heavy_processes, active_runs or defender: doctor could not read
  the machine, so it is no condition, never a contributor and not counted
  as a warning.
- possible_contributors: each warned or failed check that was read, with
  the count of each related slowdown kind (RELATED_SLOWDOWNS in
  _autopsy.py: memory, heavy_processes, defender and active_runs to
  long_tool_call, timeout and background_check; disk and python_alias to
  repeated_failure) whose start is inside the launch; zero counts are left
  out, and a check with none is not listed. A possible contributor is not
  a cause.

orphan_snapshots: the wf names of snapshots for launches not in this
archive, sorted.

checks: executions [{agent, phase, role, check, certainty, full_suite,
start, end, s, timing, background, on_critical_path, and for a
task-checks execution selected}], by_check {<check id>: {runs, inferred,
untimed, total_s}}, full_suite_runs {total, inferred, by_role: {<role>:
n}}, task_check_calls {total, by_selected: {targeted, lane, widened,
unavailable}, failed, reasons: {<code>: n}}, project_test_runs {total,
by_role: {<role>: n}, unavailable}, repairs {attempts, eligible,
by_status: {<status>: n}}, setup {skipped, no_setup_commands} and waits
{checks_via_procedure, wait_calls, stopped, improvised: {monitor_errors,
sleep_blocked, harness_output_reads}, unwaited, left_running,
background_not_waited} (the long-check procedure;
checks_via_procedure is unavailable for a run that could not have used it,
and waits is unavailable for a run without tool events). unwaited and
left_running are [{agent, file}], file the result file's name only, never
its path.
background_not_waited counts the calls that went to the background, with
an observed completion, in whose window the agent did not wait (see
background_check): no slowdown, so the run still shows work it sent to
the background.
- A check id names a configured command: <group>#<n> is
  commands.<group>[n - 1] (test#1 is commands.test[0]),
  <lane>.<group>#<n> is lane_commands.<lane>.<group>[n - 1] and
  <task>.task.<group>#<n> is task_checks.<task>.<group>[n - 1] (listed
  after the lane overrides); a command string already listed keeps its
  first id. check is that id, 'run-checks' or 'task-checks', never the
  command line or the configured command.
- An execution is a Bash call whose command line runs a configured command
  (commands.test, lint and build, the lane_commands overrides and the
  task_checks entries), run-checks or task-checks (a word whose basename
  is run-checks or task-checks, as the program of a simple command or as
  the script of python, py, bash or sh, or of a bare variable ($NAME or
  ${NAME}, such as "$python", read as an interpreter from 1.6.13), past
  its options and not given -c or -m; a line that runs both is a
  run-checks call). The line is read as shell words
  (heredoc bodies left out, a backslash before a path character read as
  '/') split into simple
  commands at ; & | && || ( ) and newlines; an & inside a redirection
  (2>&1, >&2, &>f) is no separator. A configured command runs where a
  simple command's words start with its words, both without redirections,
  leading keywords, NAME=value assignments and prefixes such as env,
  timeout DURATION or npx. The match with the most words names the
  execution (the first configured on a tie). Text that only mentions a
  check (echo "npm test", a heredoc body, bash -c "npm test") and a line
  whose quoting does not parse are not executions.
- certainty is ran or inferred. A matched simple command ran when no if,
  elif, else, then, while, until, for, case, do or function comes before
  it or leads it, and either every operator before it is ;, a newline, |
  or a background &, or the whole line is pipelines joined only by &&
  (no ;, newline, background & or ||) and the call exited 0 (a successful
  result without an 'Exit code N' line counts as 0). Anything else is
  inferred: it may not have run (cd DIR && check that failed may have
  failed at the cd).
- full_suite is ran or inferred when a simple command that exactly matches
  a commands.test entry (or a run-checks call passing '--cmd test') ran or
  only may have run, else null. For a task-checks execution it comes from
  its evidence (below): ran when a test-group command executed, else null;
  inferred without evidence.
- A task-checks execution is matched to an evidence file the helper wrote,
  <ledger>/checks/task-*.json: a JSON object whose policy has a non-empty
  string unit, a non-empty list of task ids, selected (targeted, lane or
  widened), reasons [{code, detail}], a started timestamp and a boolean
  project_test_executed, with a boolean ok and a list of results (an error
  record's is [], beside an error field). Any other file is malformed and
  never matched. The match is the unused file whose policy.unit equals the
  unit id of the agent's label, compared whole (a batch's A1-A3 never
  matches A1), with the earliest policy.started from the call to its end
  (any time from the call when its end is unknown). selected is the
  file's; with no match it is "unavailable". A matched call surely ran:
  its certainty is ran.
- start is the call. timing says how end was found: measured, the call's
  result; bound, a backgrounded call's observed completion (see
  Background runs), an upper bound on its real end; unavailable, a
  backgrounded call without an observed completion or a call without a
  result (a missing result says nothing about the finish), whose end and
  s are "unavailable".
- on_critical_path: the agent is on time.critical_path.
- by_check: runs counts the executions that ran, inferred the others;
  untimed counts the ran executions without a duration, and total_s sums
  the timed ones that ran (bound durations in it are upper bounds).
- full_suite_runs (autopsy.md's 'Check calls with a test group' and the
  summary's, the legacy count): total and by_role (run-report's roles)
  count the executions whose full_suite is ran, inferred those whose
  full_suite is inferred. A test group may be narrower than the project
  suite (a focused run-checks --cmd test, a targeted task check). total
  is a lower bound: runs through a wrapper (bash -c, a script) are not
  seen, and inferred runs may not have happened.
- task_check_calls: total counts the task-checks executions; by_selected
  splits them by selected; failed counts those whose evidence has ok
  false; reasons tallies the reason codes of the matched evidence
  (no_entry, no_files, outside_files, widen_path, forced, git_failed).
  A call without valid evidence counts as unavailable, never as 0.
- project_test_runs (autopsy.md's 'Project test runs', first on the
  summary's check line): total and by_role count the executions that ran an
  exact commands.test entry (the whole project suite): a run-checks call
  with a '--cmd test CMD' pair whose CMD is one, a task-checks execution
  whose evidence has project_test_executed true, or a configured command
  line whose full_suite is ran. unavailable counts the executions where
  that cannot be known: a task-checks call without evidence, or a project
  test run that may not have happened (inferred).
- repairs: over every launch result's final.repairs (each launch lists
  its own attempts): attempts, eligible (entries with eligible true) and
  by_status (each entry's status, "unavailable" when it has none).
  "unavailable" when no launch result has a final.repairs list (a run
  before 1.6.0).
- setup: skipped is the manifest's setup_result.setup_skipped (the lanes
  whose setup commands were skipped for having no pending work) and
  no_setup_commands the lanes with no setup commands (their
  lane_commands.<lane>.setup, else commands.setup, is empty), in manifest
  order. "unavailable" without a setup_skipped list.
- With no tool event in the whole run, executions, by_check, total,
  inferred, by_role, task_check_calls and project_test_runs are
  "unavailable"; repairs and setup do not need tool events.

autopsy.md's Checks section shows each of these counters with a one-line
definition under it.

Times are ISO 8601 UTC strings with milliseconds; durations are seconds, to
0.1. Anything that cannot be read or derived is "unavailable", never 0.

Exit 0 on success; 2 on a usage error or when RUN_FOLDER has no *.lanes.json
or no wf_* dir; 3 when a path needs converting on Windows and no Git Bash or
cygpath is found.
"""
import json
import os
import sys

sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
import _autopsy  # noqa: E402
import _shell  # noqa: E402


def main(argv):
    _shell.setup_io()
    args = argv[1:]
    quiet = "--quiet" in args
    args = [a for a in args if a != "--quiet"]
    if len(args) != 1:
        print("usage: autopsy RUN_FOLDER [--quiet]", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        folder = _shell.native_path(args[0])
    except _shell.BashNotFound as e:
        print(f"autopsy: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3
    if not os.path.isdir(folder):
        print(f"autopsy: not a directory: {folder}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if _autopsy.find_manifest(folder) is None:
        print(f"autopsy: no *.lanes.json manifest in {folder}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if not _autopsy.wf_dirs(folder):
        print(f"autopsy: no wf_* transcript dir in {folder}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    analysis = _autopsy.analyze(folder)
    out = os.path.join(folder, "autopsy.json")
    md_path = os.path.join(folder, "autopsy.md")
    _shell.write_text(out, json.dumps(analysis, indent=2) + "\n")
    _shell.write_text(md_path, _autopsy.render_markdown(analysis))
    if not quiet:
        shown = os.path.abspath(md_path).replace("\\", "/")
        sys.stdout.write(_autopsy.render_summary(analysis, shown))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
